Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:28:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Build the angles on the true obliquity of date

computeChart projected apparent sidereal time onto the mean ecliptic. GAST
carries the nutation in longitude, but the obliquity was the mean one,
evaluated at UTC centuries. On the 3,128-case grid of zodiacs.org's
preregistration (brief v1 rule 1b; audit ledger angles-houses-aspects-1 and
time-6), the ascendant then sat up to 506.8" from an ERFA arbiter at -66
degrees, and up to 14.6" within 45 degrees of the equator.

The angles now use astronomy-engine's true obliquity, e_tilt(time).tobl.
That is the mean obliquity plus the nutation in obliquity, from the same
model as the sidereal time, on TT. On the same grid against ERFA, the
ascendant gives p50 0.065", p95 0.240" and a largest of 6.36", and 0.36"
within 45 degrees. Both are inside the rule's gates of 8" and 0.5". The
midheaven's largest difference from ERFA falls from 2.25" to 0.20".

Receipts record angles "gast-and-true-obliquity". Receipts from rc.3 to rc.6
keep "gast-and-mean-obliquity" in CONVENTIONS_RC3 and are read as before. An
rc.6 receipt carrying the new token is a mixed set and is refused.

Tests:

- Twelve ERFA anchors from the site's committed arbiter, including the two
  old worst cases and +/-63, +/-65 and +/-66 degrees. Each is within the
  rule's gate. With the mean obliquity, eleven of them fail.
- The polar receipt test's pinned ascendant moves 0.16", to 0.04" from ERFA,
  where it was 0.12".

495 tests pass.

